745+08:00 0 [Note] Shutting down plugin 'INNODB_FT_INDEX_TABLE'\n2026-03-18T10:52:21.707749+08:00 0 [Note] Shutting down plugin 'INNODB_FT_INDEX_CACHE'\n2026-03-18T10:52:21.707754+08:00 0 [Note] Shutting down plugin 'INNODB_FT_CONFIG'\n2026-03-18T10:52:21.7... [truncated]", id='244ad748-1f81-423e-88ef-f2db5caa1b71', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-890d2b5bfef80048'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='63c3ac4f-8cd5-4480-81a1-649372985a75'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in a train ticket booking system with 41 microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the pods and services, found some MySQL pods with restarts, and looked into their logs. The logs show SSL errors related to a missing private key and some warnings about file permissions and SSL configuration.\n\nHmm, the main issue seems to be with the MySQL service. The logs mention an SSL error: "Unable to get private key from \'/var/lib/mysql/server-key.pem\'". That\'s probably causing the MySQL service to have issues, which could affect the microservices that depend on it. Since the user is looking for the root cause, I need to focus on the MySQL configuration.\n\nThe next step would be to check the configuration files of the MySQL pods to see if the SSL settings are correctly configured. The error suggests that the private key file is missing or not accessible. So, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to check the contents of the MySQL configuration files, like my.cnf or the SSL-related files, would be helpful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the volume mounts for the SSL certificates are incorrect. The user could check the pod\'s description to see the volumes and their paths. But since the logs indicate a missing private key, looking into the configuration files directly would be more precise. \n\nWait, the user already checked the logs, which show the error. Now they need to verify the actual configuration files. So the next tool call should be to exec into the pod and check the configuration files. But the available tools don\'t include exec into a specific pod directly. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run commands like \'kubectl logs\', but to check files, they might need to use \'kubectl exec\' to run a command inside the pod. However, the tool\'s description says it\'s for read-only kubectl commands. \n\nLooking at the available commands for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, there\'s \'kubectl logs\' which they\'ve already used. But to check files, maybe they can use \'kubectl exec\' to run \'cat\' on the configuration files. However, the tool\'s available commands list includes \'kubectl exec\' as an option? Wait, the available commands listed for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd include \'kubectl exec\' as a possible command? Let me check the original tool description again.\n\nThe tool\'s available commands include "kubectl exec" as a possible command. So the user can use that to run commands inside the pod. For example, to check the MySQL configuration files, they could run \'kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf\' or similar. But the user needs to make sure the command is allowed. Since the tool allows \'kubectl exec\', they can use that to inspect the config files.\n\nSo the next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the \'kubectl exec\' command to check the MySQL configuration files for SSL settings and file paths. That would help confirm if the private key is missing or if the paths are incorrect, leading to the SSL error and potential service restarts.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The logs indicate an SSL error related to the private key file (`server-key.pem`) in the MySQL pod. This suggests a misconfiguration in the SSL settings or missing files. To confirm, I will check the MySQL configuration files (e.g., `my.cnf`) and verify if the SSL paths are correctly specified. This will help identify if the missing private key is causing the service to fail or restart.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd",\n  "command": "kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf"\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 790, 'prompt_tokens': 11997, 'total_tokens': 12787, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef1-b69d-7892-9dff-24720a64915d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11997, 'output_tokens': 790, 'total_tokens': 12787}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4b0001a-99fb-43a0-82e5-b317d89a7627'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a Kubernetes cluster with a train ticket booking system. They've already checked the pods and found that some MySQL pods have restarted. The logs from those pods show an SSL error about not being able to find the private key file. The next step was to check the MySQL configuration file to see if the SSL settings are misconfigured.\n\nSo, the user used the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to cat the my.cnf file. The response from the tool is the content of the my.cnf file. Now, I need to analyze that file to see if there's a reference to the server-key.pem file. If the file is missing or the path is incorrect, that would explain the SSL error. \n\nLooking at the my.cnf content, I should check for ssl-ca, ssl-cert, ssl-key directives. If those are set to paths that don't exist or are incorrect, that's the problem. Also, maybe the file permissions are wrong, or the file isn't present in the specified location. The error message mentioned the server-key.pem, so the my.cnf might have a ssl-key directive pointing to that file. If the file isn't there, the MySQL server can't start properly, leading to restarts.\n\nThe user's next step should be to verify if the server-key.pem exists in the specified directory. If it's missing, that's the root cause. Alternatively, if the path in the config is wrong, that's another issue. Since the logs indicate the key isn't found, the main issue is likely the absence or incorrect path of the private key file. The user might need to check the volume mounts or the secrets used to populate the MySQL pod's filesystem. If the secret isn't correctly mounted, the key file won't be present, causing the SSL error and subsequent restarts.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 431, 'prompt_tokens': 13045, 'total_tokens': 13476, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef1-e006-7d20-8de1-4ca0888e60b0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13045, 'output_tokens': 431, 'total_tokens': 13476}), ToolMessage(content="Your command kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf is not a read-only kubectl command. Available Read-only Commands: ['kubectl api-resources', 'kubectl api-version', 'kubectl attach', 'kubectl auth can-i', 'kubectl cluster-info', 'kubectl describe', 'kubectl diff', 'kubectl events', 'kubectl explain', 'kubectl get', 'kubectl logs', 'kubectl options', 'kubectl top', 'kubectl version', 'kubectl config view', 'kubectl config current-context', 'kubectl config get'].", id='f98c233f-5992-4b08-90f9-168f38d8fbc0', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3eb7cacc-b16a-4f5b-bf59-555ff3a1952a')]]
