e logs, which show the error. Now they need to verify the actual configuration files. So the next tool call should be to exec into the pod and check the configuration files. But the available tools don\'t include exec into a specific pod directly. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run commands like \'kubectl logs\', but to check files, they might need to use \'kubectl exec\' to run a command inside the pod. However, the tool\'s description says it\'s for read-only kubectl commands. \n\nLooking at the available commands for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, there\'s \'kubectl logs\' which they\'ve already used. But to check files, maybe they can use \'kubectl exec\' to run \'cat\' on the configuration files. However, the tool\'s available commands list includes \'kubectl exec\' as an option? Wait, the available commands listed for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd include \'kubectl exec\' as a possible command? Let me check the original tool description again.\n\nThe tool\'s available commands include "kubectl exec" as a possible command. So the user can use that to run commands inside the pod. For example, to check the MySQL configuration files, they could run \'kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf\' or similar. But the user needs to make sure the command is allowed. Since the tool allows \'kubectl exec\', they can use that to inspect the config files.\n\nSo the next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the \'kubectl exec\' command to check the MySQL configuration files for SSL settings and file paths. That would help confirm if the private key is missing or if the paths are incorrect, leading to the SSL error and potential service restarts.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The logs indicate an SSL error related to the private key file (`server-key.pem`) in the MySQL pod. This suggests a misconfiguration in the SSL settings or missing files. To confirm, I will check the MySQL configuration files (e.g., `my.cnf`) and verify if the SSL paths are correctly specified. This will help identify if the missing private key is causing the service to fail or restart.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd",\n  "command": "kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf"\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 790, 'prompt_tokens': 11997, 'total_tokens': 12787, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef1-b69d-7892-9dff-24720a64915d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11997, 'output_tokens': 790, 'total_tokens': 12787}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4b0001a-99fb-43a0-82e5-b317d89a7627'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a Kubernetes cluster with a train ticket booking system. They've already checked the pods and found that some MySQL pods have restarted. The logs from those pods show an SSL error about not being able to find the private key file. The next step was to check the MySQL configuration file to see if the SSL settings are misconfigured.\n\nSo, the user used the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to cat the my.cnf file. The response from the tool is the content of the my.cnf file. Now, I need to analyze that file to see if there's a reference to the server-key.pem file. If the file is missing or the path is incorrect, that would explain the SSL error. \n\nLooking at the my.cnf content, I should check for ssl-ca, ssl-cert, ssl-key directives. If those are set to paths that don't exist or are incorrect, that's the problem. Also, maybe the file permissions are wrong, or the file isn't present in the specified location. The error message mentioned the server-key.pem, so the my.cnf might have a ssl-key directive pointing to that file. If the file isn't there, the MySQL server can't start properly, leading to restarts.\n\nThe user's next step should be to verify if the server-key.pem exists in the specified directory. If it's missing, that's the root cause. Alternatively, if the path in the config is wrong, that's another issue. Since the logs indicate the key isn't found, the main issue is likely the absence or incorrect path of the private key file. The user might need to check the volume mounts or the secrets used to populate the MySQL pod's filesystem. If the secret isn't correctly mounted, the key file won't be present, causing the SSL error and subsequent restarts.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 431, 'prompt_tokens': 13045, 'total_tokens': 13476, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef1-e006-7d20-8de1-4ca0888e60b0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13045, 'output_tokens': 431, 'total_tokens': 13476}), ToolMessage(content="Your command kubectl exec -it nacosdb-mysql-0 -n train-ticket -- cat /etc/mysql/my.cnf is not a read-only kubectl command. Available Read-only Commands: ['kubectl api-resources', 'kubectl api-version', 'kubectl attach', 'kubectl auth can-i', 'kubectl cluster-info', 'kubectl describe', 'kubectl diff', 'kubectl events', 'kubectl explain', 'kubectl get', 'kubectl logs', 'kubectl options', 'kubectl top', 'kubectl version', 'kubectl config view', 'kubectl config current-context', 'kubectl config get'].", id='f98c233f-5992-4b08-90f9-168f38d8fbc0', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-80d50c77b746a63b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3eb7cacc-b16a-4f5b-bf59-555ff3a1952a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user tried to run a command to check the MySQL configuration file but got an error because the `exec` command isn\'t allowed. The available read-only commands don\'t include `exec`, so I need to find another way.\n\nHmm, the goal is to check the MySQL configuration. Since I can\'t use `exec` to view the file directly, maybe I can look at the pod\'s configuration files through other means. Wait, maybe the pod\'s configuration is part of the Kubernetes resources. But the user already checked the pods and services, and the logs showed an SSL error related to the private key.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the MySQL configuration is stored in a ConfigMap. In Kubernetes, ConfigMaps are used to store configuration data. If the MySQL pod is using a ConfigMap for its configuration, I can check the ConfigMap to see if the SSL settings are correct.\n\nSo, the next step should be to check the ConfigMap that the MySQL pod is using. The ConfigMap might have the `my.cnf` file\'s contents. To find which ConfigMap is used, I can look at the pod\'s specifications. But since I can\'t use `exec`, maybe I can use `kubectl get configmap` to list ConfigMaps in the namespace and check their contents.\n\nWait, the user is in the `train-ticket` namespace. So, I should check for ConfigMaps in that namespace. The MySQL pod might be referencing a ConfigMap named something like `mysql-config` or similar. Once I find the ConfigMap, I can describe it to see the contents, including SSL-related settings.\n\nSo, the tool to use here is `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command `kubectl get configmap -n train-ticket`. That will list all ConfigMaps in the namespace. Then, I can check which one is associated with MySQL and view its contents. If the ConfigMap exists and has the necessary SSL configurations, that might resolve the issue. If not, it could explain the missing private key error.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: Since `kubectl exec` is not allowed, I will instead check for ConfigMaps or Secrets in the `train-ticket` namespace that might contain the MySQL configuration or SSL credentials. This will help determine if the missing `server-key.pem` or misconfigured SSL settings are due to missing or incorrect configuration data.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd",\n  "command": "kubectl get configmap -n train-ticket"\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 525, 'prompt_tokens': 12363, 'total_tokens': 12888, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef1-f7d2-7261-b95c-3c07ebaf3769-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12363, 'output_tokens': 525, 'total_tokens': 12888}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8517aeb9-5463-471c-a5b1-3d0496794db1'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a Kubernetes cluster with a train ticket booking system. They've already checked the pods and services, and found that some MySQL pods (nacosdb-mysql-0, 1, 2) have restarted. The logs from one of those pods show an SSL error related to a missing private key file (server-key.pem). \n\nThe user tried to check the MySQL configuration file (my.cnf) using kubectl exec, but that's not allowed because the available commands don't include exec. So they need another way to check the configuration. Since ConfigMaps are often used to store configuration files in Kubernetes, the next logical step is to check the ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if the MySQL configuration is stored there. \n\nBy retrieving the ConfigMaps, they can look for any that might contain the SSL configuration or the paths to the key files. If the ConfigMap is missing or misconfigured, that could explain why the server-key.pem isn't found. Additionally, Secrets might be involved if the SSL credentials are stored there, but the user hasn't checked Secrets yet. However, since the error is about a missing file, checking ConfigMaps first makes sense.\n\nSo the tool call should be to get the ConfigMaps in the train-ticket namespace. That should help determine if the configuration is correctly set up, which could resolve the SSL error and the restarts.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap -n train-ticket"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9f4cbcbe48c88e8b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 332, 'prompt_tokens': 13375, 'total_tokens': 13707, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef2-1249-7682-a5d5-1dfa77e2fd43-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap -n train-ticket'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9f4cbcbe48c88e8b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13375, 'output_tokens': 332, 'total_tokens': 13707}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                DATA   AGE\nflagd-config        1      26m\nkube-root-ca.crt    1      26m\nlocustfile-config   1      2m44s\nnacos               1      20m\nnacosdb-mysql       5      25m\nrabbitmq            1      17m\ntsdb-mysql          5      17m\n', id='2acc425c-fcd1-4a57-bbba-419e4d77c222', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9f4cbcbe48c88e8b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='55137502-cc07-42b2-a22b-ad09236c56c8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the train-ticket application, which has 41 microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the pods and services, found some MySQL pods with restarts, and looked into the logs. The logs showed an SSL error related to a missing private key file. They tried to check the MySQL config file but couldn\'t because the exec command isn\'t allowed. Then they checked ConfigMaps in the namespace and found one called nacosdb-mysql with 5 data entries.\n\nNow, the next step should be to look into the nacosdb-mysql ConfigMap to see if it contains the necessary SSL configuration or the paths to the certificate files. Since the MySQL pod is having issues with the server-key.pem, maybe the ConfigMap has the correct paths or the certificate data. By checking the ConfigMap, they can verify if the SSL settings are properly configured or if the required files are missing. If the ConfigMap doesn\'t have the necessary data, that could be the root cause. Alternatively, if the paths in the ConfigMap are incorrect, that might explain why the private key isn\'t found. So, using kubectl to describe or get the contents of the nacosdb-mysql ConfigMap would be the next logical step.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `nacosdb-mysql` ConfigMap likely contains the SSL configuration or certificate data required by the MySQL pod. By inspecting its contents, we can verify if the `server-key.pem` or other SSL-related files are misconfigured, missing, or improperly referenced. This will confirm if the SSL error is due to a configuration issue or missing secrets.  \n\ntool_call:  \n{\n  "tool": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd",\n  "command": "kubectl get configmap nacosdb-mysql -n train-ticket -o yaml"\n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 393, 'prompt_tokens': 12655, 'total_tokens': 13048, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef2-25dd-74f2-a63e-3889e5a29e38-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12655, 'output_tokens': 393, 'total_tokens': 13048}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='68e961ff-5902-4c9d-9cd6-27baea01ad17'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the nacosdb-mysql pods in the train-ticket namespace. From the logs, there was an SSL error about unable to get the private key from server-key.pem. The previous steps tried to check the MySQL configuration file but couldn't because exec isn't allowed. Then they checked ConfigMaps and found nacosdb-mysql has 5 data entries.\n\nNow, the next step is to get the details of the nacosdb-mysql ConfigMap to see if it includes the SSL configuration. The user is using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, and the last command was to get the ConfigMap in YAML format. The response would show the data entries. If the ConfigMap has entries like ssl-ca, ssl-cert, ssl-key, etc., that might indicate where the SSL files are supposed to be. If those are missing, that could explain the error. Alternatively, if the ConfigMap references secrets that aren't properly set up, that's another angle. The goal is to check if the ConfigMap contains the necessary SSL files or references to them, which would help determine if the issue is a missing file or a misconfiguration.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap nacosdb-mysql -n train-ticket -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a6c9339ca625cc90', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 288, 'prompt_tokens': 13686, 'total_tokens': 13974, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfef2-39cf-7d02-aa67-f905d58d7535-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap nacosdb-mysql -n train-ticket -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a6c9339ca625cc90', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13686, 'output_tokens': 288, 'total_tokens': 13974}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\ndata:\n  create-peers.sh: |\n    #!/bin/sh\n    set -eu\n    i=0\n    while [ $i -lt 3 ]\n    do\n      if [ $i = 0 ]\n      then\n        echo -n "nacosdb-mysql-${i}.nacosdb-mysql.train-ticket:8801"\n      else\n        echo -n ",nacosdb-mysql-${i}.nacosdb-mysql.train-ticket:8801"\n      fi\n      i=$((i+1))\n    done\n  leader-start.sh: |\n    #!/usr/bin/env bash\n    curl -X PATCH -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)" -H "Content-Type: application/json-patch+json" \\\n    --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt https://$KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST:$KUBERNETES_PORT_443_TCP_PORT/api/v1/namespaces/train-ticket/pods/$HOSTNAME \\\n    -d \'[{"op": "replace", "path": "/metadata/labels/role", "value": "leader"}]\'\n  leader-stop.sh: |\n    #!/usr/bin/env bash\n    curl -X PATCH -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)" -H "Content-Type: application/json-patch+json" \\\n    --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt https://$KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST:$KUBERNETES_PORT_443_TCP_PORT/api/v1/namespaces/train-ticket/pods/$HOSTNAME \\\n    -d \'[{"op": "replace", "path": "/metadata/labels/role", "value": "follower"}]\'\n  node.cnf: |\n    [mysqld]\n    default_storage_engine=InnoDB\n    max_connections=65535\n  server-id.cnf: |\n    [mysqld]\n    server-id=@@SERVER_ID@@\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    meta.helm.sh/release-name: nacosdb\n    meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: train-ticket\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-18T02:50:58Z"\n  labels:\n    app: nacosdb-mysql\n    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm\n    chart: mysql-1.0.0\n    heritage: Helm\n    release: nacosdb\n  name: nacosdb-mysql\n  namespace: train-ticket\n  resourceVersion: "407523"\n  uid: 576db81c-36e5-44bd-a3cb-07d82d8bff16\n', id='cb17afcd-2534-44a1-93b5-7dd19aaca758', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a6c9339ca625cc90'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f5af68b1-fef0-400e-b28c-094a1d11c83e')]]
