ait, the user found that the Secret \'nacosdb-mysql\' only has password credentials. So the SSL key isn\'t in the Secret. Therefore, the correct action is to add the \'server-key.pem\' to the Secret. But how? The user might need to create a new Secret with the SSL files or update the existing one. \n\nBut the tools available include exec_kubectl_cmd_safely. Maybe they can create a new Secret with the SSL key. However, the user hasn\'t done that yet. So the next step would be to create the Secret with the missing file. But first, maybe check if the Secret exists and if it\'s correctly configured. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check the ConfigMap to see if the SSL files are there. But the user already checked the ConfigMap \'nacosdb-mysql\' and found no SSL files. So the problem is the Secret. \n\nSo the next tool to use would be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to create or update the Secret with the \'server-key.pem\' file. But the user needs to generate the correct command. For example, using kubectl create secret generic with the file. \n\nWait, but the user might not have the file on their local machine. They might need to upload it. But the tools available don\'t include file upload. So maybe the user needs to create the Secret with the necessary files. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check the existing Secrets in the namespace to see if there\'s another one that should contain the SSL key. The user checked the \'nacosdb-mysql\' Secret but maybe there\'s another one. \n\nBut according to the previous steps, the user found that the \'nacosdb-mysql\' Secret only has password credentials. So the correct action is to add the \'server-key.pem\' to that Secret or a new one. \n\nSo the next step is to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to create a new Secret with the SSL key. The command would be something like:\n\nkubectl create secret generic nacosdb-mysql-ssl --from-file=server-key.pem=/path/to/server-key.pem\n\nBut the user needs to have the file. However, since the user can\'t interactively 